			<div class="content" id="post-content-4475418">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I can get behind the explanation of the beliefs of him as a heroic saviour being some kind of mental projection of his hopes and dreams, if that's what you were going for, but in that case I'm not sure how the second stage of infection fits into this.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I like that explanation, but as you mentioned it is inconsistent with Stage II and therefore would not be correct.</p>
<p>I had preferred to leave a certain element of ambiguity in the article both because of the element of mystery associated with the competition and because of a theme of personal taste, I like articles that can give rise to different explanations by the reader.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;or&nbsp;an&nbsp;explain?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>What occurs in this story is an event of reconfiguration of reality, with the Class D subject interviewed being the center of that entire event. In the original timeline, Andrew Bolivien becomes a researcher hired by the Foundation, dying during a Containment Breach. But someone or something, whether called the Death, the Fate or the Reality, decides to give him a second chance, and consequently live a new life. However, this life is less noticeable and less successful, has more obstacles, never becomes a scientist, that is, is a &quot;random guy&quot; as you describe. The problem is that there are subjects who somehow have memories associated with Bolivien and his actions in the original timeline, and how those are remarkable memories, they share them with others. What is the solution? To distort those memories, so that the subjects cannot remember Bolivien on good terms. However, they continue to share them, the solution is to eliminate them, at least those who have the most memories (certain Alpha subjects) (a sort of &quot;purge the data record of reality&quot;) and erase the memory of those who have the fewest memories (the Beta) because they received the story second hand.</p>
<p>Consequently, what the Foundation takes to be a memetic infection can be interpreted more like echoes of a restructuring event. The members of Site-34 speak Spanish, so they interpret the surname &quot;Bolivien&quot; as &quot;Bolívar,&quot; although it also works in the opposite sense, that is, in his new life, this subject is surnamed Bolivien, being his original surname Bolívar.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<blockquote>
<p>I also found some of the dialogue stilted and there were a few phrasing errors throughout (eg 'Have been described two classes of infected').</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is because I am not a native speaker of English, therefore after translation and even after proofreading there may be some expressions that sound strange. I will correct those details as I find them and hopefully avoid them in future works</p>

