					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jakuwoski" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3382837); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3382837&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795039" alt="Jakuwoski" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3382837)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jakuwoski" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3382837); return false;" >Jakuwoski</a></span> <span class="odate time_1580172015 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Jan 2020 00:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4481173">
									

<p>It’s an interesting explain to the article but originally I thought the SCP in another way.</p>
<p>Generally, I like the articles <em>stand alone</em> and when the anomalies are manageable, because above all, <em>in universe</em> the Foundation's staff is highly qualified, so they would be expected to respond in the best possible way.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;An&nbsp;explain</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I preferred to leave certain ambiguous elements in the article, both because of the element of mystery associated with the contest and because of personal taste. I like articles that can be interpreted in different ways by the reader.</p>
<p>What the Foundation initially takes, based on the evidence it has, as a memetic infection, turns out to be something else. There is certainly a memetic element, the anomalous origin of memories, but those memories have a tangible origin. In one version of reality, Bolívar/Bolivien is a researcher at the Foundation and dies as a result. However, <em>something</em> decides to give him a second chance, but in exchange for living a much less remarkable life, and that is the life Bolivien has. For more details, please see the answer to ModernMajorGeneral.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Secondly; there were some minor spelling/grammar issues that broke the SCP tone for me; a look over by a kind editor would fix this.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is because I am not a native speaker of English, and after the translation and even after the proofreading some details and expressions may sound strange. I would appreciate it if someone could point out these problems so that the necessary corrections can be made if corrections are allowed during the voting period.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Thirdly; the reveal of the person being real seemed unrelated to the story so far (except by name) so lessening the tension. Is this person a victim? A saboteur? A result of the virus? Any of these could work, but at the moment I can't tell which it's meant to be.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Bolivien's presence indicates that there are more than changing memories, that something in reality itself was altered and that certain echoes persist.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

