		<div class="post" id="post-4482082">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,4482082)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4482082">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cara-donnelly" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2354697); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2354697&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795039" alt="cara_donnelly" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2354697)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cara-donnelly" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2354697); return false;" >cara_donnelly</a></span> <span class="odate time_1580244744 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Jan 2020 20:52</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4482082">
									

<p>Again, I like your ideas, and I hope you keep writing :)</p>
<p>As you've indicated a desire for spelling/grammar feedback for English, I put some in a block here. My main feedback is that in a formal or technical writing style (like the SCP Foundation entries) English is surprisingly strict about how to use <strong>comparisons</strong>.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First para:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Those affected by the treatment should be observed for for a minimum of 60 days.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This should probably be &quot;exposed to the anomaly&quot; rather than &quot;affected by the treatment&quot;. As written this would suggest observing people who are successfully treated, but not observing people who are /not/ successfully treated.</p>
<p>Second para:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>of the Foundation for mentions and references about SCP-5000</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Perhaps overly strict, but this should be: &quot;mentions of and references to&quot; &#8212; these prepositions agree with the verb form of the noun, so you 'refer to' things. I am sorry that &quot;mentions of&quot; doesn't follow the rule I'm claiming exists here!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Adding, those responsible for these references</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Adding&quot; should be &quot;Additionally&quot; or &quot;Furthermore&quot;. There's some unintentional ambiguity in &quot;those responsible&quot;, as &quot;responsible&quot; can mean &quot;supposed to be in charge of&quot;, &quot;actually in charge of&quot;, or &quot;the author of&quot;. I think you mean &quot;the authors of these references&quot;.</p>
<p>Third paragraph: Rereading this paragraph I /really/ like the efficient project management the Foundation is deploying here &#8212; deploying weak amnestics, estimating the needed innoculation for immunity, and giving a timeline for completion. This is really nice detail. No suggested fixes here.</p>
<p>Fourth para:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5000 corresponds to a memetic phenomenon that has been described as affecting only employees of the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Described as&quot; suggests there is someone else doing the describing, but this is the documentation for SCP-5000, so this is confusing. Similarly, &quot;Corresponds to&quot; suggests there's some system being compared to the Foundation database e.g. the Global Occult Initiative's database. I suggest &quot;SCP-5000 is a memetic phenomenon that has only been observed to affect employees of the Foundation&quot;.</p>
<p>Still fourth para:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This phenomenon is characterized both by the induction of anomalous memories in those affected and by retroactive alteration of the local registration systems, making them consistent with what was indicated by those affected, regardless of whether this alteration contradicts data in the public domain or information described in other registration systems.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is a long chain of relative clauses that require the reader to keep several nested comparisons in mind while reading it. As Andrés Bolívar has not been introduced yet, &quot;local registration systems&quot; doesn't have the context needed to make it clear that it's personnel databases that are affected. I would split this after the comma to make two shorter sentences, each simpler to read and maybe change &quot;registration systems&quot; to &quot;personnel databases&quot;. Lots of things can have registrations (e.g. cars) but only people are in personnel databases.</p>
<p>Fifth para:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Even if there is a certain level of divergence in the details, the accounts tend to agree in their general aspects, even among subjects who have not had direct communication or contact with each other.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The word &quot;Even&quot; here is again making a comparison against something which isn't present. This would be fine in informal writing as it's implied you're comparing to 'normality', but is unusual in technical writing where it is important that the reader get a very precise understanding.</p>
<p>Sixth para:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Inspections of the registry systems of sectors of Site-34 not affected by the phenomenon, as well as of external registry systems and ARCA (Anomalous Contingency Rescue Archive), have made it possible to conclude that Junior Researcher Bolívar is not a current or historic staff member and no events described by SCP-5000 affected personnel occurred at any Foundation Site during December 2018.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence has &quot;subject confusion&quot;. Let's split this up into two, then look at the first half in more detail:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Inspections of the registry systems of sectors of Site-34 not affected by the phenomenon, as well as of external registry systems and ARCA (Anomalous Contingency Rescue Archive).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The comma after 'phenomenon' here reads like a list, so it sounds like the sentence is saying &quot;Inspections aren't affected by the phenomenon, and inspections aren't affected by external registry systems either&quot;. When you have a list of nouns like this it's sensible to keep them close to each other in the sentence. However, here the nouns are quite long, so that doesn't seem to be a good option. Splitting a sentence into shorter sentences is always a good choice, so I suggest &quot;Inspection of the Site 34 registry systems are not affected by the phenomenon. External registry systems and ARCA (Anomalous Contingency Rescue Archive) are also unaffected.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These inspections have made it possible to conclude that Junior Researcher Bolívar is not a current or historic staff member and no events described by SCP-5000 affected personnel occurred at any Foundation Site during December 2018.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>After the split, the second half of this sentence is fine. By adding two splits in this sentence you get three sentences that are both simpler.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I've reviewed the main text for main points of spelling and grammar but not the collapsibles. I hope it's helpful, but please let me know if I've failed in that at any point.</p>

