<p>Thanks for your reading ImperiumRomanun.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;About&nbsp;the&nbsp;article</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;an&nbsp;explain</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In my original idea, the phenomenon was due to a reality restructuring event. In the original timeline, there was a researcher named Andres Bolivar who, due to actions beyond his duties, died during a containment gap. However, &quot;something&quot; (whether called Death, Fate or some other higher force) decided that he should not die on that occasion, giving him a second chance, this new life being, however, much less remarkable than the previous one. There is a problem and it is that there are still &quot;echoes&quot; of Bolívar's actions, memories of those who either witnessed or heard about his actions, and in the face of the incongruity that is generated, that same force that gave him a second chance is forced to correct that &quot;failure&quot;. First, it seeks to distort Bolivar's memories, so that he is forgotten, and since this is not effective, it eliminates the subjects who have the most memories (who are the ones who initiate the dissemination of history) and induces amnesia in those who heard it, in the period corresponding to when the incident occurred (similar to a computer registry cleanup)</p>
<p>As the Foundation does not know these antecedents, it takes the phenomenon as a conventional memetic infection, especially virulent and transmissible through the story, with certain dangerous cognitive elements, which is why it takes the measures it considers appropriate.</p>
<p>However, the interpretation changes when Bolivien appears, who shares the characteristics described for Bolívar, and there the questions arise: What is his relationship with the phenomenon? Is he perhaps Bolivar? Why is he there?</p>
<p>The differences in the name of the Junior Researcher and the Class D are due to the way memory works, it tends to be inaccurate, and at the same time to the language differences of the staff of Site-34 (they are Spanish speaking, so the name and surname sounded like &quot;Andrés Bolívar&quot;)</p>
<p>One interpretation given by several readers that I liked a lot is that the phenomenon is due to the unfulfilled dreams and aspirations of Bolivien, who by becoming a Class D without hope of freedom or a better life, dreams of a new life in a faraway place, far from crime and violence, being someone respectable. And because as a child he wanted to be a scientist, that form took on its aspiration.</p>
</div>
